<% sandbox = playground_path() %>
<div class="wrap">
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
    <h1>EUI's score viewer</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      <strong>eui-score</strong> is the engraver behind the <code>score()</code> widget of the EUI catalogue: a
      subset of the LilyPond language, laid out as LilyPond lays it out, drawn as paths; the same source played
      as a MIDI file and printed as a PDF.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="doc-layout">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#what">What it is</a></li>
        <li><a href="#show">Showing a score</a></li>
        <li><a href="#width">Width and systems</a></li>
        <li><a href="#marks">Highlighted notes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#a4">On A4 paper</a></li>
        <li><a href="#midi">Listening: MIDI</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pdf">Printing: PDF</a></li>
        <li><a href="#server">On a Soli server</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rust">From Rust</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fidelity">Faithful to LilyPond</a></li>
        <li><a href="#speed">Speed</a></li>
        <li><a href="#editor">The editor</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <div class="prose">
      <section class="doc-section" id="what">
        <h2>What it is<a class="anchor" href="#what">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          A score, in EUI, is not an image: it is a list of paths. Staff lines, stems and bar lines are
          rectangles; everything curved — noteheads, clefs, accidentals, flags, beams, slurs that thicken towards
          the middle — is an outline cut from <strong>Emmentaler</strong>, LilyPond's music font. Titles and tempo
          marks are drawn the same way, from the letters of <strong>TeX Gyre Schola</strong>. No font travels and
          no text node is involved: every glyph lands at the size and the place the engraving wants, to the
          hundredth of a pixel.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Rust crate <code>eui-score</code> is a function-for-function port of the engraver written in Soli
          (<code>eui_builders_score.sl</code>). Its golden tests hold it to the original on the 26 pieces of the
          corpus: every path to the hundredth of a pixel, the MIDI and the PDF to the byte, and its 44 refusals
          word for word, position included. Compiled to WebAssembly, it also runs in the browser:
          <a href="<%= embed_path() %>">two tags are enough to show scores on any website</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Refuse rather than draw it wrong.</strong> A source the engraver cannot set is refused with the
          character where it stopped — <code>score: … (at character N)</code> — never half drawn.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="show">
        <h2>Showing a score<a class="anchor" href="#show">#</a></h2>
        <p>In an EUI view, the score is a widget like any other:</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>score("\\relative c' { \\key g \\major \\time 3/4 g'4( a b) | d2. \\bar \"|.\" }",
      {"width": 640})</code></pre>
        <%- score_figure(hello, {"name": "syntax/hello", "sandbox": sandbox, "source": "none"}) %>
        <p>
          It is drawn as canvases: one for the titles, one per system. An edit redraws and resends only the system
          it touches — each system keeps its paths under a signature of what it draws.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>width</code></td><td>600</td><td>Width in logical px; bars are broken into systems that fit it.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>staff_space</code></td><td>8</td><td>Logical px between two staff lines: the size of the engraving.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>colour</code></td><td><code>text.default</code></td><td>The ink, a colour role: the score follows the light or dark theme.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>marks</code></td><td><code>{}</code></td><td>Item number (notes and rests from 0, as a string) → the role to draw it in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>page</code></td><td>absent</td><td><code>"a4"</code> lays the music out on A4 sheets at LilyPond's staff size.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="width">
        <h2>Width and systems<a class="anchor" href="#width">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The same source at two widths: the engraver breaks the bars into lines, justifies every full line and
          leaves the last at its natural width. Lines after the first open with their bar number; a key or time
          change at the start of a line is announced at the end of the one before.
        </p>
        <%- score_figure(narrow, {"source": "none", "caption": "\"Little Waltz\", width 440, staff_space 5.5"}) %>
        <% wide_caption = "The same, width 1000, staff_space 6.5" %>
        <%- score_figure(wide, {"name": "piano/waltz", "sandbox": sandbox, "source": "closed", "caption": wide_caption}) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="marks">
        <h2>Highlighted notes<a class="anchor" href="#marks">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>marks</code> draws some items again in another colour: that is how an editor shows its selection,
          and a player the note that is sounding. Items are the notes and rests, numbered from 0 in source order.
          Here item 1 (the A) and item 3 (the D):
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>score(src, {"width": 640, "marks": {"1": "accent.base", "3": "warning.base"}})</code></pre>
        <%- score_figure(marked, {"source": "none"}) %>
        <p>
          The highlight is a copy of the item's paths laid over the untouched system: when the playhead moves,
          only that layer changes. On Beethoven's Fifth in full score, a playhead step sends 3.5 to 8.9 KB instead
          of the system's 267 KB.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="a4">
        <h2>On A4 paper<a class="anchor" href="#a4">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          With <code>"page": "a4"</code>, the music is laid out as LilyPond would at its default staff size (20 pt,
          a staff space of 1.764 mm): 10 mm side margins and 5 mm at the top, the first system indented 15 mm,
          12 spaces from staff to staff, LilyPond's page breaks. Full pages are spread to the bottom margin; pages
          after the first carry their number at the outer top corner. <code>score()</code> then answers a column
          of sheets <code>width</code> px wide.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>score(src, {"width": 794, "page": "a4"})</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="midi">
        <h2>Listening: MIDI<a class="anchor" href="#midi">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>score_midi(src, opts)</code> writes the same source as a Standard MIDI File, as LilyPond's
          <code>\midi</code> block would: time signatures, keys and tempos; one note per tie; dynamics as volumes
          by LilyPond's own values, a hairpin moving the volume note by note, accents louder and staccatos
          shorter. One track per staff, each on its own channel with its <code>midiInstrument</code>. On the EUI
          gallery pieces its notes, times and lengths are LilyPond 2.24's, note for note.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>bytes = score_midi(src, {"bpm": 96, "beat": 4, "program": 0})   # a list of bytes</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>bpm</code> and <code>beat</code> give a tempo to a source that has none; <code>program</code> a
          General MIDI instrument to a staff that names none. Every score on this site has a <strong>Play</strong>
          button that plays this file.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="pdf">
        <h2>Printing: PDF<a class="anchor" href="#pdf">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>score_pdf(src)</code> writes the A4 pages as a PDF: the same paths as the screen, as vectors at
          five points a staff space, every letter an outline so no font is embedded, the title in the document's
          information and in each page's footer with its number. The result is an ASCII string: a
          <code>file_save</code> handler can answer with it as it is.
        </p>
        <p>
          Try it: <a href="<%= example_pdf_path("ensembles", "symphony-5") %>">Beethoven's Fifth as a PDF</a>, or
          <a href="<%= example_pdf_path("piano", "minuet-in-g") %>">the Minuet in G</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="server">
        <h2>On a Soli server<a class="anchor" href="#server">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The native engraver is available to every Soli application (the <code>eui</code> feature, in the
          published binaries) through four functions. The <code>score()</code> widget takes them when they are
          there and engraves in Soli otherwise; both draw the same.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>eui_score_layout(src, width, layout)</code></td><td>The layout, <code>width</code> in staff spaces: systems, pages, and for every bar its items with their x and heads — enough to place and hit-test. A refused source answers <code>{"error": message}</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>eui_score_bands(laid, sp, opts, total)</code></td><td>A page as bands, one canvas each, at <code>sp</code> px a staff space: <code>paths</code> in the ink, <code>lit</code> for the items of <code>opts["marks"]</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>eui_score_midi(src, opts)</code></td><td>The MIDI file, as a list of bytes.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>eui_score_pdf(src, title)</code></td><td>The A4 PDF, as a string.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          A path is an array: <code>[1, ink, x, y, w, h, 0]</code> for a rectangle, <code>[5, ink, commands…]</code>
          for an outline, where <code>0</code> is a move, <code>1</code> a line, <code>2</code> a quadratic,
          <code>3</code> a cubic and <code>4</code> a close. This site's server-side pages use nothing else: each
          score is engraved per request and turned into SVG in a few dozen lines.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>laid = eui_score_layout(src, width / staff_space, {})
return {"error": laid["error"]} if laid.has_key("error")

total = (laid["height"] * staff_space).ceil()
eui_score_bands(laid, staff_space, {"colour": "ink"}, total).each do |band|
  band["paths"].each do |shape|
    # shape[0] == 1: a &lt;rect&gt;, otherwise a &lt;path d="M… L… Q… C… Z"&gt;
    # moved down by band["y"]
  end
end</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="rust">
        <h2>From Rust<a class="anchor" href="#rust">#</a></h2>
        <p>The crate has one dependency, <code>serde_json</code>, to read the font data.</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>let eng = eui_score::engrave(src, 80.0, &amp;eui_score::Layout::default())?;  // 80 staff spaces wide
let (paths, ranges) = eui_score::paths(&amp;eng, 8.0, 0)?;   // page 0, 8 px a staff space
let bands = eui_score::bands(&amp;eng, 8.0, 0, total_px)?;   // the same, as bands

let a4 = eui_score::engrave_a4(src)?;                     // the music on A4
let midi: Vec&lt;u8&gt; = eui_score::midi(src, &amp;eui_score::MidiOptions::default())?;
let pdf: String = eui_score::pdf(src, "Title")?;</code></pre>
        <p>
          Every length inside is in staff spaces, y down, a staff's top line at 0 and its bottom one at 4; paths
          are in pixels at <code>sp</code> px a staff space. A whole note (<code>WHOLE</code>) is 26,880 ticks: a
          double-dotted thirty-second lands on a whole number, and so does any of them in a triplet, quintuplet,
          sextuplet or septuplet.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="fidelity">
        <h2>Faithful to LilyPond<a class="anchor" href="#fidelity">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The rules are LilyPond's wherever they can be stated in a line, and the thicknesses are its defaults at
          staff size 20:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>spacing from the most common shortest note, 1.2 spaces more for each doubling;</li>
          <li>stems of 3.5 spaces that reach the middle line;</li>
          <li>eighths beamed by the metre, a beam placed so its stems average their ideal length, then quantised to sit, straddle or hang on a line;</li>
          <li>slurs that level their ends before arching over a note in the way, at the height <code>height-limit · 2/π · atan(π/2 · ratio / height-limit · width)</code>;</li>
          <li>staff line 0.1 space, stem 0.13, beam 0.48, thin bar line 0.19, thick bar line 0.6.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Set beside LilyPond 2.24 at the same staff size, EUI's three reference pieces come out within 2 % of its
          widths.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="speed">
        <h2>Speed<a class="anchor" href="#speed">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Measured on the exposition of Beethoven's Fifth (12 systems, 10,518 paths at 900 px), against the
          original Soli engraver:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>eui-score</th><th>Soli engraver</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Engraving</td><td><strong>1.9 ms</strong></td><td>338 ms</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Drawing the paths</td><td><strong>2.4 ms</strong></td><td>639 ms</td></tr>
              <tr><td>MIDI file</td><td><strong>1.4 ms</strong></td><td>182 ms</td></tr>
              <tr><td>PDF of the A4 pages</td><td><strong>47 ms</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          That is the difference between an edit that redraws a symphony in the next frame and one that takes a
          fifth of a second. <a href="<%= example_path("ensembles", "symphony-5") %>">See it engraved →</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="editor">
        <h2>The score editor<a class="anchor" href="#editor">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          EUI's demo application (<code>examples/demo-app</code>) serves an editor built on this engraver: A–G
          writes a note, 2–7 are the durations as MuseScore numbers them, the arrows move and select, a click
          selects a note and a second click moves it. The LilyPond source beside the staff can be edited or pasted;
          a note written across a bar line is split and tied for you.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Score</strong>: titles, clef, key, metre and tempo, for the whole piece or from the selected bar;</li>
          <li><strong>Bars</strong>: repeats (<code>[</code> and <code>]</code>), line and page breaks;</li>
          <li><strong>Expression</strong>: dynamics, hairpins, articulations, text above or below;</li>
          <li><strong>Tracks</strong>: one instrument per track, volume, pan, mute, bracket or brace grouping, and the full score under the sheet;</li>
          <li>triplets (Ctrl+3) and sextuplets (Ctrl+6), sixty undos, <strong>PDF</strong> and <strong>MIDI</strong> export, playback mixed to <code>.wav</code> by the server.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          To write without installing anything, this site's <a href="<%= sandbox %>">playground</a> engraves your
          LilyPond as you type.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
